PART 2 — Special Ability Animations

2.1 Specials must feel more significant

Special abilities should have noticeably longer, more dramatic animations than ordinary attacks.

The game already has a cast/release animation system, so preserve and build upon it rather than replacing it.

The three qualifying Specials should retain their existing deliberate buildup:

#0001 — Unlimited Void

Maintain its recognizable casting animation and release timing. The ability should feel powerful and deliberate before the paralysis activates.

#0001 — Hollow Purple

Maintain its extended buildup and projectile release. This should continue to feel like one of #0001’s most significant moves.

#0003 — Mystical Palm Technique

Maintain its longer kneeling healing animation. Its longer vulnerability period is part of the technique’s identity.

These should remain clearly more committed animations than regular attacks.

Do not arbitrarily slow the three Specials further if the existing timings already achieve the intended effect.

2.2 Make non-Special abilities faster where necessary

Moves that are no longer considered Special should have ordinary-attack pacing.

However, some are already fast enough and must not receive unnecessary speed adjustments.

Move	Required animation change
#0001 Red	Keep existing speed
#0001 Maximum Blue	Keep existing speed
#0002 Whirlwind	Keep existing speed
#0003 Cherry Blossom Impact	Speed up its animation

Cherry Blossom Impact is the main timing adjustment required.

Currently, its animation clips are defined in js/data/characters/0003.js using:

attack3_cast: 10
attack3_release: 15

The cast contains four frames, and the release contains five frames.

This gives Cherry Blossom Impact a comparatively long total animation.

Increase the animation playback rate so that the attack feels quicker and more responsive, while retaining every original sprite frame and its distinctive ground impact.

A reasonable starting point would be:

attack3_cast: 20
attack3_release: 20

This would bring its combined animation duration to approximately 0.45 seconds, rather than approximately 0.73 seconds.

Use the game’s frame-aligned timing conventions. Verify that the eruption still activates at the correct release frame.

Adjust within a similarly quick range if necessary to preserve smooth gameplay.

Important requirements:

* Do not delete animation frames.
* Do not skip the essential anticipation or impact pose.
* Keep visual animation and gameplay timing synchronized.
* Retain its burst, damage, launch, and hit properties.
* Ensure the character becomes free to act when the animation actually finishes.
* Ensure interruptions and cooldown behavior remain correct.

The attack should still look powerful but no longer require a disproportionately long buildup for a normal move.

2.3 Special animation indicator — Orange Exclamation Mark

Implement a new visual indicator that appears directly above a character’s head while the character performs a genuinely Special ability.

Design:

Display a clearly visible orange exclamation mark:

!

It should visually communicate that the character is executing an exceptionally powerful or unusual ability.

Visual requirements:

* Bright, saturated orange, for example #FF9F1C.
* Dark outline or shadow for visibility against bright and dark stages.
* Clean and legible at different screen sizes.
* Centered horizontally above the active character.
* Positioned high enough to avoid covering the character’s face or sprite artwork.
* Avoid overlapping existing player labels, Energy bars, or cooldown indicators.
* Scale appropriately with the game’s Canvas rendering and device pixel ratio.
* Maintain a crisp appearance consistent with Alva’s existing visual style.

A very subtle pulse or floating movement is acceptable but not required. The exclamation mark itself is the priority.

It must not become excessively large, flashy, or distracting.

2.4 When the indicator appears

The exclamation mark should appear immediately when a qualifying Special ability successfully begins.

It must remain visible while the Special animation plays, including the casting and release animation phases.

It should disappear as soon as the character finishes performing that Special.

Expected examples:

Unlimited Void

1. Character begins the Unlimited Void cast.
2. Orange exclamation mark appears above its head.
3. The mark stays visible throughout the cast and release animation.
4. The mark disappears once the fighter has finished the Special animation.

Hollow Purple

The mark appears during the buildup, remains through the character’s release animation, and disappears when the animation ends. It must not remain merely because the projectile is still traveling.

Mystical Palm Technique

The mark appears when healing begins, remains throughout the kneeling and healing animation, and disappears once the character finishes.

The indicator must also disappear immediately if an ability is interrupted, canceled, or otherwise terminated.

It must not appear simply because a move is on cooldown.

2.5 When the indicator must NOT appear

Never show the orange exclamation mark during:

* Red
* Maximum Blue
* Whirlwind
* Cherry Blossom Impact
* Any other non-Special attack
* Standard movement
* Jumping or dashing
* Normal shielding or deflecting
* Idle animations
* Cooldown periods after a Special has finished

The indicator is associated with the active Special animation, not the attack’s damage tier, projectile properties, or cooldown.

2.6 Implementation architecture

Investigate:

* js/game/arena.js
* js/game/rendering/fighter-status.js
* js/game/fighters/fighter.js
* js/game/combat/technique.js
* js/game/rendering/sprite-animator.js

The current game uses a Canvas 2D renderer.

Implement the indicator in the existing rendering pipeline rather than introducing floating DOM elements over the game canvas.

Recommended approach:

1. Use the new shared Special classification helper.
2. Determine which move the fighter is actively performing.
3. Check whether that move is classified as Special.
4. Draw the mark using the fighter’s current rendered position.
5. Remove the mark automatically when the active Special state ends.

The current fighter.technique state and its active technique ID should provide the necessary information for the three existing Specials.

However, design the helper and rendering logic so future Specials can also use other supported move execution types.

Prefer leveraging markerAnchor(), statusTop(), or related positioning utilities in Arena, rather than creating a separate incompatible positioning system.

The indicator must work for both players and CPU-controlled characters.

It must also work in:

* Regular battles
* Custom battles
* Watch Mode
* Practice Ground
* Mirror matches

The indicator should render correctly even when both fighters activate Specials around the same time.

Respect reduced-motion preferences if the mark is animated.

